Unix utility 'script'

The Unix utility 'script' starts a shell on a pseudoterminal and records everything the
shell shows into a file, as the script of BSD does. The session looks and behaves as it would
on the terminal 'script' was called from, and ends when the shell exits.

The file holds the bytes exactly as the shell wrote them, escape sequences included, so
showing it on a terminal replays the session. It is written once the session ends, replacing
a file of the same name. If no file is given, the session is recorded into 'script.out'.

Inside the session, the keys go to the shell as they are typed, and Ctrl+C ends the command
the shell is running, not the shell. The shell sees a window of the same size as the
terminal 'script' was called from.

USAGE:

script [file]

Example of use:

script session.txt

It records a session into 'session.txt'. Leave it with 'exit'.

For more information, enter 'script ?' or 'script --help'.

HISTORY

This command appeared in the Hexagonix SonamCore development branch (2026).

